<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tharu people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tharu are an indigenous people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a> lowlands along the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Long resistant to malaria, they farmed the forested plains when few others lived there, and they speak Indo-Aryan Tharu languages. From the 1950s malaria eradication and migration from the hills cost them much land, and many were bonded labourers until the kamaiya system was abolished in 2000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tharu-people.jpg" alt="Tharu people" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Punya · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tharu_Mahila.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
